WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.955
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.957 --> 00:00:10.460
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.461 --> 00:00:12.846
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.848 --> 00:00:15.165
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:15.165 --> 00:00:17.584
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.586 --> 00:00:19.807
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.807 --> 00:00:21.570
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.965 --> 00:00:24.361
I'm Alastair Budge, and today

00:00:24.363 --> 00:00:26.923
it's part two of our three-part

00:00:26.923 --> 00:00:29.222
mini-series on dinosaurs.

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:32.053
In case you missed it, in part

00:00:32.055 --> 00:00:34.706
one, we heard the story of the Bone

00:00:34.711 --> 00:00:37.808
Wars, the twenty-year feud between

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:40.631
Edward Drinker Cope and Othniel

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.199
Charles Marsh that gave the world

00:00:43.201 --> 00:00:46.265
Stegosaurus, Triceratops and Diplodocus.

00:00:46.563 --> 00:00:48.640
In part three, we'll go back

00:00:48.642 --> 00:00:50.866
sixty-six million years to the

00:00:50.866 --> 00:00:53.481
day an asteroid hit the Earth, and

00:00:53.483 --> 00:00:56.172
follow what happened, hour by hour.

00:00:56.524 --> 00:00:59.565
But today, in part two, we are going

00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:02.183
to be talking about how science

00:01:02.206 --> 00:01:05.130
got dinosaurs wrong, spectacularly

00:01:05.132 --> 00:01:08.311
wrong, for more than a hundred years.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.164
So, let's not waste a minute

00:01:11.166 --> 00:01:13.100
and get right into it.

00:01:14.535 --> 00:01:17.361
It is New Year's Eve, 1853,

00:01:17.363 --> 00:01:20.082
in a park in south London.

00:01:21.026 --> 00:01:23.213
Twenty-one gentlemen in evening

00:01:23.215 --> 00:01:25.542
dress are sitting down to dinner.

00:01:26.085 --> 00:01:28.680
There is soup, there is roast meat,

00:01:28.682 --> 00:01:31.497
there is plenty of wine, and there are

00:01:31.514 --> 00:01:34.917
speeches and songs late into the night.

00:01:35.663 --> 00:01:37.686
Not particularly unusual for the

00:01:37.688 --> 00:01:39.836
31st of December, you might think.

00:01:40.145 --> 00:01:42.186
But there is something

00:01:42.188 --> 00:01:44.599
unusual about this dinner.

00:01:45.804 --> 00:01:48.176
The table has been set

00:01:48.178 --> 00:01:50.441
up inside a dinosaur.

00:01:51.568 --> 00:01:54.892
To be precise, it is inside the mould

00:01:54.894 --> 00:01:58.037
of a life-sized concrete Iguanodon,

00:01:58.541 --> 00:02:01.090
one of more than thirty giant model

00:02:01.092 --> 00:02:03.639
animals being built for the grounds

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:06.159
of the Crystal Palace, the great glass

00:02:06.161 --> 00:02:08.346
exhibition building that is being

00:02:08.358 --> 00:02:10.768
rebuilt in this corner of London.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:15.077
At the head of the table is Professor

00:02:15.079 --> 00:02:17.480
Richard Owen, the most famous

00:02:17.509 --> 00:02:20.205
anatomist in Britain, and the man

00:02:20.207 --> 00:02:22.900
who invented the word "dinosaur".

00:02:23.782 --> 00:02:26.380
He is sitting, appropriately enough,

00:02:26.382 --> 00:02:29.412
roughly where the animal's brain would be.

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:34.206
Eleven years earlier, in 1842,

00:02:34.208 --> 00:02:37.655
Owen had looked at the handful of

00:02:37.721 --> 00:02:40.341
giant fossil reptiles then known

00:02:40.343 --> 00:02:43.125
from England and decided that they

00:02:43.136 --> 00:02:45.740
belonged together, in their own group.

00:02:46.759 --> 00:02:48.921
He called them Dinosauria:

00:02:48.923 --> 00:02:51.416
the "fearfully great lizards".

00:02:52.662 --> 00:02:55.325
And the Crystal Palace models were

00:02:55.327 --> 00:02:57.988
his chance to show the public what

00:02:58.034 --> 00:03:00.120
these creatures had looked like.

00:03:01.199 --> 00:03:03.608
There was just one small difficulty.

00:03:04.219 --> 00:03:06.020
Nobody actually knew.

00:03:07.064 --> 00:03:10.068
At this point, not a single complete

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:13.239
dinosaur skeleton had ever been found.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:16.928
Owen had teamed up with a brilliant

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.386
sculptor called Benjamin Waterhouse

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:22.882
Hawkins, but the two of them were working

00:03:22.884 --> 00:03:26.033
from fragments: a few teeth, some leg

00:03:26.046 --> 00:03:29.386
bones, pieces of jaw and rib and spine.

00:03:30.666 --> 00:03:33.628
From these fragments, they built what

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:36.350
can only be described as monsters.

00:03:37.244 --> 00:03:40.163
Their Iguanodon is a vast, scaly,

00:03:40.165 --> 00:03:43.348
four-legged creature, something like

00:03:43.349 --> 00:03:46.056
a rhinoceros crossed with a crocodile,

00:03:46.058 --> 00:03:48.976
with a small horn on the end of its nose.

00:03:50.269 --> 00:03:52.673
The models caused a sensation.

00:03:53.393 --> 00:03:56.025
Tens of thousands of Victorians

00:03:56.027 --> 00:03:57.893
came to stare at them.

00:03:58.642 --> 00:04:00.864
They were the first dinosaurs the

00:04:00.866 --> 00:04:03.489
public had ever seen, and they fixed an

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:06.740
image of these animals in the popular

00:04:06.742 --> 00:04:09.805
imagination: giant, slow, lumbering

00:04:09.817 --> 00:04:12.431
reptiles from a primitive world.

00:04:13.575 --> 00:04:14.977
They are still there today, by

00:04:14.979 --> 00:04:16.380
the way, standing around their

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.266
lake in Crystal Palace Park.

00:04:18.839 --> 00:04:20.126
They are wonderful.

00:04:20.563 --> 00:04:21.109
Scary.

00:04:21.632 --> 00:04:23.468
Beautiful, in their own way.

00:04:24.213 --> 00:04:26.240
They are also wrong in

00:04:26.242 --> 00:04:28.082
almost every detail.

00:04:29.247 --> 00:04:32.777
Take that horn on the Iguanodon's nose.

00:04:33.557 --> 00:04:35.751
Any self-respecting six-year-old

00:04:35.753 --> 00:04:38.219
dinosaur enthusiast would be able to

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:41.306
tell you an Iguanodon has no such thing.

00:04:42.443 --> 00:04:45.107
Indeed, when more complete skeletons

00:04:45.109 --> 00:04:47.771
were dug up in Belgium a few decades

00:04:47.771 --> 00:04:51.207
later, it turned out that the "horn" was

00:04:51.209 --> 00:04:54.729
actually a spike from the animal's thumb.

00:04:55.443 --> 00:04:57.890
So Iguanodon didn't have a horn

00:04:57.892 --> 00:05:00.258
on its nose, but spikes on its

00:05:00.271 --> 00:05:02.791
hands, most probably for defence.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.099
What's more, it could rear up and

00:05:06.101 --> 00:05:08.261
move on its back legs: a world

00:05:08.262 --> 00:05:10.382
away from the rhinoceros-like

00:05:10.384 --> 00:05:12.355
beast standing in the park.

00:05:13.342 --> 00:05:15.787
The Crystal Palace monster was,

00:05:15.789 --> 00:05:18.469
in effect, a piece of guesswork, a

00:05:18.469 --> 00:05:20.820
puzzle that had been put together,

00:05:20.822 --> 00:05:22.757
but mostly in the wrong way.

00:05:24.192 --> 00:05:26.410
Fast forward a few decades, to

00:05:26.412 --> 00:05:28.775
the early twentieth century, and

00:05:29.073 --> 00:05:31.749
museums had much more to work with.

00:05:32.425 --> 00:05:34.587
Their halls were filling up with

00:05:34.589 --> 00:05:37.019
enormous skeletons from the American

00:05:37.019 --> 00:05:39.211
West, the ones that Cope and Marsh

00:05:39.213 --> 00:05:41.402
had been blasting out of the rock.

00:05:42.558 --> 00:05:45.169
And a strange consensus had

00:05:45.171 --> 00:05:47.587
settled over the science.

00:05:48.993 --> 00:05:51.929
Dinosaurs, the textbooks said, were

00:05:51.931 --> 00:05:54.613
reptiles, so they must have been

00:05:54.655 --> 00:05:57.270
cold-blooded, like modern lizards

00:05:57.272 --> 00:06:00.043
and crocodiles: slow-moving animals

00:06:00.197 --> 00:06:02.775
that depended on the sun for their

00:06:02.777 --> 00:06:05.126
body heat, and needed to lounge

00:06:05.145 --> 00:06:07.558
around for extended periods as if to

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:09.903
recharge their batteries every day.

00:06:11.561 --> 00:06:13.988
This cold-blooded reptile theory

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:16.566
was more than just a technicality;

00:06:17.144 --> 00:06:19.403
everything else about what people

00:06:19.405 --> 00:06:21.867
knew about dinosaurs flowed from it.

00:06:22.931 --> 00:06:25.019
A cold-blooded animal, like a

00:06:25.021 --> 00:06:27.251
lizard or a crocodile, gets its

00:06:27.251 --> 00:06:29.204
heat from the world around it.

00:06:29.847 --> 00:06:31.670
In the cool of the morning

00:06:31.672 --> 00:06:33.143
it is slow and stiff.

00:06:33.337 --> 00:06:35.176
It warms itself in the sun,

00:06:35.178 --> 00:06:37.220
it can manage a short burst of

00:06:37.247 --> 00:06:39.336
effort, and then it has to rest.

00:06:40.102 --> 00:06:41.752
In exchange, it needs

00:06:41.754 --> 00:06:43.560
remarkably little food.

00:06:44.097 --> 00:06:47.112
A crocodile can go months without eating.

00:06:48.452 --> 00:06:51.887
A warm-blooded animal, like a bird or a

00:06:51.889 --> 00:06:55.233
mammal, runs its own internal furnace.

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:57.427
It can be active all day and

00:06:57.429 --> 00:06:59.021
all night, in any weather.

00:06:59.582 --> 00:07:03.455
But the furnace has to be fed, constantly,

00:07:03.457 --> 00:07:07.235
and that means a warm-blooded animal eats

00:07:07.254 --> 00:07:11.492
far more and lives at a far faster pace.

00:07:12.168 --> 00:07:14.960
So whether dinosaurs were cold-blooded

00:07:14.962 --> 00:07:17.679
or warm-blooded isn't a technicality.

00:07:17.968 --> 00:07:20.745
It decides how fast they moved, how much

00:07:20.747 --> 00:07:23.522
they ate, how quickly they grew, whether

00:07:23.523 --> 00:07:25.779
they hunted or simply waited, whether

00:07:25.781 --> 00:07:28.340
they had energy to look after their young.

00:07:28.566 --> 00:07:30.741
It decides almost everything

00:07:30.743 --> 00:07:32.373
about how they lived.

00:07:33.467 --> 00:07:35.403
Decide that they were cold-blooded,

00:07:35.405 --> 00:07:37.340
and you have decided that they were

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:39.805
slow, hungry only occasionally,

00:07:39.807 --> 00:07:41.714
and more or less stupid.

00:07:42.717 --> 00:07:44.784
And for a hundred years,

00:07:44.786 --> 00:07:47.196
that was the consensus view.

00:07:48.075 --> 00:07:50.442
The giant ones, like Diplodocus

00:07:50.444 --> 00:07:52.732
and Brontosaurus, were thought

00:07:52.786 --> 00:07:55.182
to be so heavy that they could

00:07:55.184 --> 00:07:57.338
barely walk on land at all.

00:07:58.468 --> 00:08:00.562
Museums and painters showed them

00:08:00.564 --> 00:08:02.459
standing up to their necks in

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:05.062
swamps, letting the water carry

00:08:05.064 --> 00:08:08.000
their weight, munching soft plants.

00:08:08.793 --> 00:08:11.080
The predators were seen as

00:08:11.082 --> 00:08:13.630
sluggish, dim-witted killers.

00:08:14.309 --> 00:08:15.692
The plant-eaters were

00:08:15.694 --> 00:08:17.273
slower and dimmer still.

00:08:18.294 --> 00:08:20.989
Stegosaurus, it was noted, had a

00:08:20.991 --> 00:08:24.020
brain the size of a walnut in a body

00:08:24.033 --> 00:08:26.939
the size of a van, and for decades

00:08:26.941 --> 00:08:30.187
scientists seriously discussed whether

00:08:30.192 --> 00:08:32.921
it needed a kind of "second brain" near

00:08:32.923 --> 00:08:35.440
its hips just to move its back legs.

00:08:36.171 --> 00:08:38.175
It was thought to be so stupid,

00:08:38.177 --> 00:08:39.855
essentially, that it could

00:08:39.861 --> 00:08:41.720
hardly physically function.

00:08:43.432 --> 00:08:46.460
Everywhere, in every reconstruction,

00:08:46.462 --> 00:08:49.236
the dinosaurs dragged their heavy

00:08:49.237 --> 00:08:51.911
tails along the ground behind them,

00:08:51.913 --> 00:08:54.585
like massive, exhausted crocodiles.

00:08:56.201 --> 00:08:59.990
And hanging over all of it was one

00:08:59.992 --> 00:09:03.779
big idea: dinosaurs were failures.

00:09:04.349 --> 00:09:07.033
They were seen as nature's first,

00:09:07.035 --> 00:09:10.043
clumsy attempt at large land animals.

00:09:10.347 --> 00:09:13.538
Too big, too slow, too stupid.

00:09:14.526 --> 00:09:17.060
When something better came along, the

00:09:17.062 --> 00:09:19.458
story went, they were doomed, which

00:09:19.493 --> 00:09:22.349
perhaps explained why they were extinct.

00:09:23.502 --> 00:09:26.601
And this extinction, however it happened,

00:09:26.603 --> 00:09:29.398
was simply the verdict they deserved.

00:09:30.718 --> 00:09:33.394
For about fifty years, from

00:09:33.396 --> 00:09:36.467
roughly the 1910s to the 1960s,

00:09:36.863 --> 00:09:38.899
this picture barely moved.

00:09:40.014 --> 00:09:42.940
Palaeontology itself had become a bit

00:09:42.942 --> 00:09:46.103
of a backwater, a dull corner of science

00:09:46.383 --> 00:09:48.649
where little was expected to change.

00:09:49.341 --> 00:09:51.373
The animals in the museum halls

00:09:51.375 --> 00:09:53.208
were spectacular, certainly.

00:09:53.635 --> 00:09:55.607
But as science, they were

00:09:55.609 --> 00:09:58.209
considered more or less finished.

00:09:58.627 --> 00:10:00.734
Dead ends, fully explained.

00:10:01.796 --> 00:10:05.494
This all changed in the summer of 1964,

00:10:05.496 --> 00:10:09.097
when a Yale palaeontologist named John

00:10:09.144 --> 00:10:12.148
Ostrom was prospecting for fossils in

00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:15.152
the dry badlands of southern Montana.

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:19.458
On the slope of a mound, he and his

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:22.492
assistant spotted a large clawed

00:10:22.504 --> 00:10:25.162
hand sticking out of the rock.

00:10:26.689 --> 00:10:28.695
As they dug, they uncovered

00:10:28.697 --> 00:10:31.369
something that made no sense at all.

00:10:32.804 --> 00:10:34.810
It was a predatory dinosaur,

00:10:34.812 --> 00:10:36.243
but not a giant one.

00:10:37.497 --> 00:10:40.653
This animal was about three metres long,

00:10:40.655 --> 00:10:43.808
and they estimated it would have weighed

00:10:43.849 --> 00:10:46.504
roughly as much as an adult human.

00:10:47.717 --> 00:10:51.378
It had long, grasping arms, a light body,

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:54.861
and a long tail stiffened by bony rods,

00:10:55.206 --> 00:10:57.730
which they thought must have functioned

00:10:57.732 --> 00:11:00.059
as a sort of natural balancing pole.

00:11:01.512 --> 00:11:04.301
And on the second toe of each

00:11:04.303 --> 00:11:07.378
foot, it carried a huge, curved,

00:11:07.582 --> 00:11:10.189
sickle-shaped claw, held up off

00:11:10.191 --> 00:11:12.796
the ground like a folded knife.

00:11:14.128 --> 00:11:16.444
Ostrom named it Deinonychus,

00:11:16.446 --> 00:11:18.760
which means "terrible claw".

00:11:20.138 --> 00:11:22.269
Then Ostrom took the anatomy

00:11:22.271 --> 00:11:24.781
seriously, and asked what kind of

00:11:24.781 --> 00:11:26.807
animal it was actually describing.

00:11:27.819 --> 00:11:30.803
A claw like that is a weapon, and

00:11:30.805 --> 00:11:33.878
to use it, the animal has to stand

00:11:33.899 --> 00:11:36.871
on one leg and slash with the other.

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:38.858
That's hard.

00:11:39.163 --> 00:11:40.841
It requires a lot of energy.

00:11:41.518 --> 00:11:43.508
The animal must have been quick.

00:11:44.028 --> 00:11:45.777
It's not the sort of thing

00:11:45.779 --> 00:11:47.526
that a slow, cold-blooded,

00:11:47.575 --> 00:11:49.754
lumbering swamp reptile could do.

00:11:50.626 --> 00:11:54.022
It was a dinosaur, that much was clear,

00:11:54.024 --> 00:11:56.896
but it looked and behaved nothing

00:11:56.896 --> 00:11:58.762
like a dinosaur was supposed to.

00:11:59.874 --> 00:12:02.426
Ostrom also noticed that several

00:12:02.428 --> 00:12:05.296
Deinonychus skeletons had been found

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:08.076
lying around a single, much larger

00:12:08.078 --> 00:12:11.168
plant-eater, and wondered whether these

00:12:11.173 --> 00:12:14.375
animals hunted in packs, like wolves.

00:12:15.919 --> 00:12:19.138
He published his description in 1969,

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:22.357
and in the comparatively sleepy world

00:12:22.368 --> 00:12:25.277
of palaeontology, it was a bombshell.

00:12:26.548 --> 00:12:29.183
If Deinonychus was fast and active,

00:12:29.185 --> 00:12:31.592
what else had science got wrong?

00:12:32.971 --> 00:12:34.911
Researchers began to

00:12:34.913 --> 00:12:37.044
re-examine everything.

00:12:38.028 --> 00:12:40.481
They looked at thousands of preserved

00:12:40.483 --> 00:12:43.066
dinosaur trackways, and saw that almost

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:46.030
none of them showed a tail-drag mark.

00:12:47.042 --> 00:12:49.714
So dinosaurs must have walked with

00:12:49.716 --> 00:12:52.464
their tails held up off the ground.

00:12:53.895 --> 00:12:56.444
And Ostrom had a student, a young

00:12:56.446 --> 00:12:58.993
man named Robert Bakker, who took

00:12:58.993 --> 00:13:01.254
his teacher's careful arguments and

00:13:01.256 --> 00:13:03.385
turned the volume all the way up.

00:13:03.936 --> 00:13:06.314
Bakker was loud, bearded,

00:13:06.316 --> 00:13:08.597
combative and brilliant.

00:13:09.905 --> 00:13:12.408
Where Ostrom was cautious, Bakker

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:15.139
declared, flatly, that the dinosaurs

00:13:15.331 --> 00:13:17.956
were warm-blooded, energetic animals,

00:13:17.958 --> 00:13:20.865
more like birds and mammals than lizards;

00:13:21.295 --> 00:13:24.184
that the textbooks were wrong; and that

00:13:24.186 --> 00:13:27.222
a century of science had made assumptions

00:13:27.424 --> 00:13:29.745
that were fundamentally incorrect.

00:13:30.865 --> 00:13:33.779
In 1975 he wrote a famous magazine

00:13:33.781 --> 00:13:36.865
article that gave the whole movement

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:39.998
its name: the Dinosaur Renaissance.

00:13:40.872 --> 00:13:44.024
And Bakker came armed with evidence.

00:13:44.899 --> 00:13:47.669
Cut a dinosaur bone open, he argued,

00:13:47.671 --> 00:13:50.593
and under the microscope it looks like

00:13:50.594 --> 00:13:53.670
the bone of a fast-growing bird, packed

00:13:53.672 --> 00:13:56.510
with blood vessels, and nothing like

00:13:56.522 --> 00:13:59.282
the slow, dense bone of a crocodile.

00:14:00.253 --> 00:14:03.063
Count the predators and prey in a fossil

00:14:03.065 --> 00:14:05.452
bed, and the proportions look like

00:14:05.469 --> 00:14:08.505
lions on the African savannah, animals

00:14:08.507 --> 00:14:11.381
with big appetites and fast engines.

00:14:12.693 --> 00:14:15.004
The old guard fought back, and

00:14:15.006 --> 00:14:17.624
the argument over warm versus cold

00:14:17.628 --> 00:14:20.314
blood ran bitterly for decades, at

00:14:20.316 --> 00:14:23.317
conferences, in journals, occasionally

00:14:23.323 --> 00:14:25.736
even erupting into shouting matches.

00:14:26.591 --> 00:14:29.437
But the centre of gravity had shifted.

00:14:30.238 --> 00:14:32.969
By the 1980s, the swamp-bound,

00:14:32.971 --> 00:14:36.065
tail-dragging beast was on the way

00:14:36.065 --> 00:14:39.599
out, and a new animal was taking its

00:14:39.601 --> 00:14:43.329
place: upright, dynamic, tail held out

00:14:43.342 --> 00:14:46.388
stiff and straight, built for movement.

00:14:46.903 --> 00:14:48.514
The sort of dinosaur you see

00:14:48.516 --> 00:14:50.241
today in children's toy shops.

00:14:51.571 --> 00:14:53.869
You can see the change in the museums.

00:14:54.607 --> 00:14:56.867
Skeletons that had stood in the old

00:14:56.869 --> 00:14:59.128
pose for the best part of a century

00:14:59.447 --> 00:15:02.264
were taken apart and remounted:

00:15:02.266 --> 00:15:04.899
bodies pitched forward, heads

00:15:04.899 --> 00:15:08.020
low, tails swept up into the air.

00:15:09.118 --> 00:15:11.670
The dinosaurs in the great halls

00:15:11.672 --> 00:15:13.982
today are the same bones your

00:15:13.997 --> 00:15:16.526
great-grandparents might have seen, just

00:15:16.528 --> 00:15:18.991
arranged in a completely different way.

00:15:20.303 --> 00:15:22.705
There was one more idea still

00:15:22.707 --> 00:15:25.024
waiting its turn, and it was

00:15:25.184 --> 00:15:26.423
older than all the others.

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:30.748
Back in 1861, two years after Darwin

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:33.770
published On the Origin of Species,

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:38.039
quarrymen in Germany had split open a slab

00:15:38.041 --> 00:15:41.403
of limestone and found a creature that

00:15:41.417 --> 00:15:45.723
seemed to be half bird and half reptile.

00:15:46.490 --> 00:15:48.777
It had wings and feathers, both

00:15:48.779 --> 00:15:51.581
beautifully preserved, but also teeth,

00:15:52.117 --> 00:15:55.588
clawed fingers and a long bony tail.

00:15:56.773 --> 00:15:59.800
It was named Archaeopteryx, the "ancient

00:15:59.802 --> 00:16:02.902
wing", and Darwin's great defender Thomas

00:16:02.929 --> 00:16:06.824
Huxley saw at once what it implied.

00:16:07.886 --> 00:16:10.856
The skeleton, feathers aside, looked

00:16:10.858 --> 00:16:13.496
almost identical to the skeleton

00:16:13.713 --> 00:16:16.202
of a small predatory dinosaur.

00:16:17.245 --> 00:16:20.269
Birds, Huxley suggested, might

00:16:20.271 --> 00:16:23.092
be descended from dinosaurs.

00:16:24.449 --> 00:16:26.492
At the time, it was little more

00:16:26.494 --> 00:16:28.338
than an interesting thought.

00:16:29.225 --> 00:16:31.690
A few people argued about it, and then

00:16:31.692 --> 00:16:34.090
it was forgotten for the best part of

00:16:34.090 --> 00:16:37.109
a century, buried under the picture

00:16:37.111 --> 00:16:39.870
of the slow, cold swamp reptile.

00:16:40.483 --> 00:16:42.548
A bird is quick, warm and clever.

00:16:43.031 --> 00:16:44.397
Nobody could see what it

00:16:44.399 --> 00:16:45.876
had to do with a dinosaur.

00:16:47.136 --> 00:16:48.663
Ostrom could.

00:16:49.714 --> 00:16:52.600
In the 1970s he put the bones of an

00:16:52.602 --> 00:16:55.569
Archaeopteryx next to the bones of a

00:16:55.689 --> 00:16:58.921
Deinonychus and showed, joint by joint,

00:16:58.923 --> 00:17:02.402
that they were built on the same plan: the

00:17:02.403 --> 00:17:04.858
same wrist, the same shoulder, the same

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:07.501
hips, the same long arms and clawed hands.

00:17:08.881 --> 00:17:11.006
His conclusion was simple:

00:17:11.008 --> 00:17:12.641
birds are dinosaurs.

00:17:13.021 --> 00:17:15.690
Living, flying, singing dinosaurs.

00:17:17.183 --> 00:17:20.993
If that was true, it changed everything.

00:17:21.581 --> 00:17:23.436
It meant that the dinosaurs

00:17:23.438 --> 00:17:25.498
had never completely died out.

00:17:25.909 --> 00:17:28.969
It meant that the model for a dinosaur

00:17:28.971 --> 00:17:31.706
was no longer a lizard but a bird:

00:17:32.217 --> 00:17:34.579
warm, quick, feathered, possibly

00:17:34.581 --> 00:17:37.532
brooding its eggs and feeding its young.

00:17:38.508 --> 00:17:41.037
And it meant that palaeontologists

00:17:41.039 --> 00:17:43.342
had a living dinosaur to study.

00:17:44.103 --> 00:17:46.639
Everything we can learn about how

00:17:46.641 --> 00:17:49.175
birds grow, breathe and behave is

00:17:49.177 --> 00:17:51.367
a window onto animals that died

00:17:51.369 --> 00:17:53.627
out sixty-six million years ago.

00:17:54.652 --> 00:17:56.728
But there was a catch.

00:17:57.554 --> 00:17:58.448
Where were the feathers?

00:17:59.519 --> 00:18:01.394
Archaeopteryx had them, of course.

00:18:01.664 --> 00:18:03.968
But Archaeopteryx was already

00:18:03.970 --> 00:18:06.033
a bird, or close enough to

00:18:06.034 --> 00:18:08.262
one that nobody was surprised.

00:18:09.189 --> 00:18:11.348
What Ostrom's critics wanted was

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:13.439
a feather on something that was

00:18:13.522 --> 00:18:16.365
unmistakably a dinosaur: a running,

00:18:16.367 --> 00:18:19.371
two-legged predator like Deinonychus.

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:22.665
And that was a problem, because

00:18:22.667 --> 00:18:24.625
feathers almost never survive.

00:18:25.422 --> 00:18:27.571
Bones turn to stone, which is

00:18:27.573 --> 00:18:29.646
why we have so many of them.

00:18:30.195 --> 00:18:32.332
Feathers rot away in days,

00:18:32.334 --> 00:18:34.797
long before a fossil can form.

00:18:36.202 --> 00:18:38.258
Archaeopteryx had only kept its

00:18:38.260 --> 00:18:40.313
feathers because it died in the

00:18:40.314 --> 00:18:42.893
fine, still mud of a lagoon, and

00:18:42.895 --> 00:18:45.392
that kind of luck is very rare.

00:18:46.407 --> 00:18:49.708
So the critics could always say: fine, you

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:52.931
say dinosaurs are birds, but show us one.

00:18:53.025 --> 00:18:54.699
Show us a dinosaur with feathers.

00:18:56.135 --> 00:18:58.832
For twenty years, nobody could.

00:18:59.815 --> 00:19:01.792
Then came a farmer's field

00:19:01.794 --> 00:19:03.846
in a place called Liaoning.

00:19:05.343 --> 00:19:08.261
Liaoning is an area in north-eastern

00:19:08.263 --> 00:19:11.504
China, near the border with North Korea.

00:19:12.335 --> 00:19:14.695
It is farming country, and for

00:19:14.697 --> 00:19:17.526
years local farmers had been digging

00:19:17.527 --> 00:19:20.393
up beautifully preserved fossils:

00:19:20.395 --> 00:19:23.346
fish, insects, early mammals, even

00:19:23.350 --> 00:19:25.511
whole flocks of primitive birds.

00:19:27.081 --> 00:19:30.504
The rocks there formed from fine volcanic

00:19:30.506 --> 00:19:33.510
ash settling into ancient lakes, and

00:19:33.843 --> 00:19:36.502
anything that died in those lakes was

00:19:36.504 --> 00:19:39.090
preserved down to the finest detail.

00:19:39.769 --> 00:19:42.366
It was a sort of prehistoric Pompeii.

00:19:44.024 --> 00:19:47.496
In 1996, Chinese scientists announced

00:19:47.498 --> 00:19:50.969
a discovery from these rocks: a small

00:19:51.350 --> 00:19:53.869
predatory dinosaur, about the size

00:19:53.871 --> 00:19:56.463
of a turkey, preserved so well that

00:19:56.475 --> 00:19:59.022
the stone had captured not just its

00:19:59.024 --> 00:20:01.787
bones but the soft tissue around them.

00:20:02.788 --> 00:20:06.502
Running down its neck, back and tail

00:20:06.504 --> 00:20:10.319
was a coat of simple, downy feathers.

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:14.510
It was named Sinosauropteryx, and when

00:20:14.512 --> 00:20:17.247
a photograph of the fossil was passed

00:20:17.271 --> 00:20:19.585
around at a palaeontology conference

00:20:19.587 --> 00:20:21.963
in New York that autumn, John Ostrom,

00:20:22.298 --> 00:20:24.992
who was by then in his late sixties,

00:20:24.994 --> 00:20:27.386
said he was in a state of shock.

00:20:28.487 --> 00:20:31.040
He had spent thirty years arguing,

00:20:31.042 --> 00:20:33.818
from bones alone, that dinosaurs were

00:20:33.822 --> 00:20:36.301
birds, or rather, birds were dinosaurs.

00:20:36.809 --> 00:20:40.319
And here was a dinosaur with feathers.

00:20:41.805 --> 00:20:44.353
And Liaoning kept giving.

00:20:45.352 --> 00:20:47.717
Over the following years, the quarries

00:20:47.719 --> 00:20:49.646
of north-eastern China produced

00:20:49.653 --> 00:20:52.464
feathered dinosaurs by the dozen:

00:20:52.466 --> 00:20:55.189
some with simple fuzz, some with

00:20:55.206 --> 00:20:56.944
full wing feathers on their arms.

00:20:57.403 --> 00:20:59.134
Even one little glider,

00:20:59.136 --> 00:21:01.015
called Microraptor, which

00:21:01.015 --> 00:21:03.259
had wings on all four limbs.

00:21:04.366 --> 00:21:07.093
Nor was it only the small ones.

00:21:08.401 --> 00:21:10.865
In 2012, Chinese scientists

00:21:10.867 --> 00:21:13.968
described Yutyrannus, a nine-metre

00:21:14.028 --> 00:21:16.489
cousin of Tyrannosaurus rex,

00:21:16.491 --> 00:21:19.302
covered in long, downy feathers.

00:21:20.673 --> 00:21:22.898
Fossils elsewhere joined in.

00:21:23.709 --> 00:21:26.955
A dinosaur called Oviraptor had been named

00:21:26.957 --> 00:21:30.202
"the egg thief" back in the 1920s, because

00:21:30.229 --> 00:21:32.854
its skeleton was found lying on top of a

00:21:32.856 --> 00:21:35.610
nest of eggs it was assumed to be robbing.

00:21:36.577 --> 00:21:39.269
In the 1990s, new specimens of

00:21:39.271 --> 00:21:42.141
a very close relative showed the

00:21:42.156 --> 00:21:44.952
same kind of animal crouched over

00:21:44.954 --> 00:21:47.579
the same kind of nest, forearms

00:21:47.804 --> 00:21:50.265
spread protectively over the eggs.

00:21:51.184 --> 00:21:53.110
The eggs were its own.

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:56.382
The "egg thief" had died brooding

00:21:56.384 --> 00:21:59.308
its nest, sitting on its eggs like a

00:21:59.309 --> 00:22:02.133
hen, and it had sat there faithfully

00:22:02.135 --> 00:22:05.036
through whatever sandstorm buried it.

00:22:06.093 --> 00:22:09.381
Then, in 2010, scientists discovered

00:22:09.383 --> 00:22:12.395
that fossil feathers can preserve

00:22:12.632 --> 00:22:15.392
melanosomes, the microscopic structures

00:22:15.394 --> 00:22:17.657
that give feathers their colour.

00:22:18.598 --> 00:22:20.920
For the first time, the colour of

00:22:20.922 --> 00:22:23.383
a dinosaur stopped being guesswork.

00:22:24.343 --> 00:22:26.530
Sinosauropteryx, it turns out,

00:22:26.532 --> 00:22:28.717
was a redhead: ginger-coloured

00:22:28.724 --> 00:22:30.972
on top, white underneath, with

00:22:30.974 --> 00:22:33.369
a ginger-and-white striped tail.

00:22:34.360 --> 00:22:36.183
So what does the modern

00:22:36.185 --> 00:22:38.324
picture actually look like?

00:22:38.951 --> 00:22:41.632
Dinosaurs walked tall, tails held

00:22:41.634 --> 00:22:44.556
out behind them, built for movement.

00:22:45.286 --> 00:22:47.179
Many were warm-blooded,

00:22:47.181 --> 00:22:49.237
or something close to it.

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:52.146
Many of them, perhaps most of the

00:22:52.148 --> 00:22:54.275
predatory ones, were feathered,

00:22:54.635 --> 00:22:56.555
and some were vividly coloured.

00:22:57.333 --> 00:23:00.333
They nested in colonies, brooded their

00:23:00.335 --> 00:23:03.569
eggs and, in at least some species, cared

00:23:03.572 --> 00:23:06.922
for their young; one duck-billed dinosaur

00:23:06.924 --> 00:23:09.700
discovered in Montana in the 1970s

00:23:09.961 --> 00:23:12.831
was named Maiasaura, "the good mother

00:23:12.833 --> 00:23:15.623
lizard", because its nesting grounds

00:23:15.774 --> 00:23:18.121
were full of babies that had clearly

00:23:18.123 --> 00:23:20.533
been fed and looked after for months.

00:23:21.655 --> 00:23:24.945
And far from being nature's failures,

00:23:24.947 --> 00:23:27.790
dinosaurs dominated life on land

00:23:28.037 --> 00:23:30.854
for around 160 million years.

00:23:31.677 --> 00:23:33.583
And some of them are still here.

00:23:34.490 --> 00:23:36.788
The sparrows outside your window,

00:23:36.790 --> 00:23:39.156
the chickens on your dinner table,

00:23:39.195 --> 00:23:41.524
the ten thousand or so species of

00:23:41.526 --> 00:23:43.711
living birds: every one of them

00:23:44.153 --> 00:23:46.197
is a dinosaur, as directly and

00:23:46.199 --> 00:23:48.240
literally as you are a mammal.

00:23:49.136 --> 00:23:51.228
Which perhaps means the English

00:23:51.230 --> 00:23:53.859
language owes these animals an apology.

00:23:54.806 --> 00:23:56.602
We use "dinosaur" to mean

00:23:56.604 --> 00:23:58.829
something that failed to adapt.

00:23:59.633 --> 00:24:02.134
The real animals adapted so well,

00:24:02.136 --> 00:24:04.560
for so long, that they are still

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:06.738
here, twittering away, a hundred

00:24:06.740 --> 00:24:08.779
and sixty million years later.

00:24:10.580 --> 00:24:12.808
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:24:12.810 --> 00:24:15.453
episode on how we got dinosaurs wrong.

00:24:15.680 --> 00:24:17.355
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:24:17.357 --> 00:24:19.126
and that you've learnt something new.

00:24:19.433 --> 00:24:21.528
Next up, in part three, we'll go back

00:24:21.530 --> 00:24:23.397
to the last day of the Cretaceous

00:24:23.425 --> 00:24:25.568
period, and follow what happened,

00:24:25.570 --> 00:24:27.906
from the moment a rock the size of a

00:24:27.917 --> 00:24:30.461
mountain hit the Earth, to the long dark

00:24:30.463 --> 00:24:32.877
winter that followed, and the unlikely

00:24:32.878 --> 00:24:35.124
survivors who inherited the planet.

00:24:36.019 --> 00:24:38.601
You've been listening to English Learning

00:24:38.603 --> 00:24:40.995
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:24:41.472 --> 00:24:44.085
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:24:44.087 --> 00:24:46.492
I'll catch you in the next episode.
